Compile a Curry module to a Python script
=========================================

This example writes the compiled form of a Curry module to a Python script
with curry.save, and then runs that script.


1. What the example shows about functional logic programming
-------------------------------------------------------------

The module wildcard_matching.curry matches a string against a pattern with
the wildcards ? and *.  The function tails is non-deterministic: it returns
every suffix of a string.  The matcher calls a set function, set2 aux, on
the string and the pattern, and tests whether the set of results is empty.
The goal main checks ten cases and is True when every case agrees.


2. What the example shows about calling Curry from Python
----------------------------------------------------------

The script go.py imports the module and saves it:

    curry.save(wildcard_matching, 'wildcard_matching.py', goal='main')

The saved file is a Python script that holds the generated code of the
module.  The goal argument records the goal to evaluate.  When the script
runs, it imports the Sprite runtime, loads the module, and prints every
value of main.  Without the goal argument the script loads the module and
runs nothing.

The saved form belongs to one backend.  On the Python backend it is Python.
On the C++ backend curry.save writes C++ source, so the example runs on the
Python backend only.


3. How to run
-------------

Run "make stage" from the root of the repository first.  Then run

    ./run

The run script uses the Python backend.  It runs go.py and then the saved
script, wildcard_matching.py.  The saved script is a build product and is
not checked in.


4. Expected output
------------------

The file expected.out holds the output that ./run is meant to produce:

    % python go.py
    Loading and compiling wildcard_matching.curry
    Saving the program to wildcard_matching.py with goal main
    % python wildcard_matching.py
    True

The last line is the value of main.

On the current Python backend the saved script prints nothing.  Every
function in the saved file is a stub that fails, so main has no value.  The
test tests/unit_examples.py lists this example as a known failure until
curry.save is fixed.  The program itself is right: its goal main evaluates
to True on both backends when the module is imported and evaluated directly.


5. Limits
---------

A warm run of ./run took 0.8 s.  The first run also compiles the module
with the Curry front end, which takes several seconds more.
